    "result_text": "Both findings addressed; build is green.\n\n## What I changed\n\n**Finding 1 (agreed) \u2014 magic offsets in `Owner`.** Introduced `CHK_WIDTH`/`HASH8_WIDTH`/`FY_WIDTH` constants plus a derived `MEMBER_ID_SUFFIX_WIDTH`, documented in one place as the layout counterpart to `AssignMemberId`'s `region + fy + hash8 + chk` construction. `getFiscalYear()` and `getLocality()` now compute their segment boundaries from those constants instead of the bare `11`/`9` literals, and both use the same `base.length() > MEMBER_ID_SUFFIX_WIDTH` guard \u2014 removing the puzzling `>= 11` vs `> 11` discrepancy (a well-formed base always has a non-empty REGION, so requiring the region to be present is the correct single condition for both).\n\n**Finding 2 (agreed) \u2014 redundant `getPrimaryIdentifier()`.** The \"customerCode today, memberId later\" seam has served its purpose, so I removed the method and pointed the audit event at `owner.getMemberId()` directly, and updated the `OwnerCreatedEvent` javadoc to reference `getMemberId()`. No lasting semantic seam was needed, so the indirection is gone rather than kept.\n\nVerification: `./mvnw -o test` \u2192 `BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`; the owner still audits as `memberId=NSW2616E6609E3`, confirming the segment-parsing getters are unchanged in behaviour.",
